<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Havel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Havel is a river in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 330 kilometres long, a right tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. It flows south from the Mecklenburg Lake District through Berlin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spandau">Spandau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, then west past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brandenburg an der Havel</a>, forming many lakes. Its basin was home to the Slavic Hevelli and became the core of the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/havel.jpg" alt="Havel" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pierre Dalbéra from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Havel_%C3%A0_Potsdam_(36928106751).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Slovakia declared sovereignty in July, <b>Havel</b> resigned, and the Federal Assembly voted in November to dissolve the federation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Brandenburg began as a frontier march on the eastern edge of the Holy Roman Empire, in lands settled by Slavic peoples such as the Hevelli on the <b>Havel</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Margrave Gero died in 965 his huge march was split, and the northern part, between the Elbe and the Oder north of the <b>Havel</b>, became the Northern March.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-march">Northern March</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto I founded the bishopric of Havelberg in 946 or 948 at a stronghold on the <b>Havel</b>, to convert the Slavs of the Northern March.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-havelberg">Prince-Bishopric of Havelberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hevelli, also called Stodorani, were West Slavic settlers of the <b>Havel</b> lands west of modern Berlin from the 7th or 8th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-the-hevelli">Principality of the Hevelli</a></li></ul></section>
